sections.s2_technical:
When a URL contains `?role=user&role=admin`, there is no universal rule in the HTTP specification for which value wins. Each technology makes its own choice. Java Servlet's `request.getParameter("role")` returns the first value, so it returns `user`. But `request.getParameterValues("role")` returns the array `["user", "admin"]`. Spring MVC, when you bind a single `@RequestParam String role`, typically takes the first value, but when you bind `@RequestParam List<String> role` you get both. A gateway written in a different language may take the last value. This inconsistency is the whole vulnerability.

The classic exploit path is a security filter that checks one occurrence and business logic that reads another. Suppose an API gateway validates the request by reading the last `role` value and sees `user`, allowing the request. Then the backend reads the first value with `getParameter` and also might behave differently, or the reverse. Where the validation layer and the execution layer pick different occurrences, the attacker crafts the request so the guard sees the safe value and the worker sees the dangerous one.

The first remediation is to reject duplicates outright. If a parameter that should appear once appears more than once, return `400 Bad Request`. In a Servlet filter you call `request.getParameterValues(name)` and if the array length is greater than one for a single-valued parameter, you reject. This is the strongest option because it removes the ambiguity entirely.

```java
String[] roles = request.getParameterValues("role");
if (roles != null && roles.length > 1) {
    // duplicate parameter — reject rather than guess which wins
    response.sendError(400, "Duplicate parameter not allowed: role");
    return;
}
```

The second remediation is canonicalization — if you must accept duplicates, collapse them to a single deterministic value and use that everywhere. Pick first-wins or last-wins, document it, and make every layer use the same rule. The danger is not duplicates themselves; it is different layers choosing differently.

The third remediation is strict schema validation. Validate the whole request against an explicit schema using bean validation on a typed DTO, so `role` is a single enum value and anything else fails. When Spring binds `@RequestParam Role role` to an enum and the request sends two values, binding fails cleanly rather than silently choosing one.

A senior nuance is that HPP also appears in query strings forwarded to downstream services. If your service builds a downstream URL by concatenating user input, an attacker can inject `&admin=true` through a polluted parameter. Always URL-encode and whitelist forwarded parameters rather than passing them through blindly.

